1. Mandatory RTO Documentation:
Before embarking on your vehicle relocation journey, ensure you have all essential documents from the RTO (Regional Transport Office). These documents include:

   a. Vehicle Registration Certificate (Form 29)
   b. Vehicle Inspection Report (Form 30)
   c. No Objection Certificate (Form 26)
   d. Vehicle Fitness Certificate (Form 27)

2. Electronic Way Bill (e-Way Bill):
In India, the e-Way Bill system has been implemented to streamline transportation of goods across states. When moving your vehicle, you'll need to generate an e-Way Bill for inter-state transportation. To create an e-Way Bill, follow these steps:

   a. Visit the GST Portal (https://www.gst.gov.in/) and register or log in if you already have an account.
   b. Click on the "Generate e-Way Bill" button and select the appropriate options.
   c. Provide the necessary details, including:
      i. Sender's (or the originating state) GSTIN
      ii. Receiver's (or the destination state) GSTIN
      iii. Vehicle Description (Make, Model, etc.)
      iv. Date of Movement
      v. Quantity of Vehicle(s)
   d. Upload relevant documents, such as the invoice, bill of landing, or commercial invoice.
   e. Confirm and generate the e-Way Bill, ensuring to keep the unique Bill Number for future reference.

3. Goods and Services Tax (GST) Compliance:
GST ensures a harmonized taxation system across India. As a vehicle owner, it's essential to be aware of GST requirements for inter-state vehicle transportation:

   a. Ensure your selected packers and movers are registered for GST.
   b. Calculate GST based on the cost of services, including transportation charges and any additional services provided.
   c. Pay GST to the service provider and request them to provide a proper GST invoice detailing the GST amount paid.

4. Packers and Movers Services:
